he trichotomy  of control which is  while certainly it is true that some of  these systemic factors  impact our lives on a day-to-day basis  our ability to influence them is  dramatically more limited than our  mindset  our goals etc and so that would be a  very  pragmatic approach to distinguishing the  two  i would love you to unpack that a little  bit more well in the sense that  the for for example if we believe  that we are not as happy as we could be  both  because we have set goals for ourselves  that  are uh counterproductive and and make us  less happy  and because of inequality  at the broad economic level our ability  to  impact the latter is far more limited i  mean certainly we can say  i want to be the best activist i can so  that our elected officials reduce income  inequality but ultimately  the ability of one person to influence  that is is relatively limited  our ability to say i'm going to set  different goals for myself in my life  that is something we control entirely  and  and and can immediately use potentially  to improve our level of satisfaction  so that's a great point and i would say  that's you know you link that to stocism  i would also say that's  quite in common with a lot of the others  as well so maybe the ones that are  you keep cropping up over again in  different philosophies we start to get  it  the truth you know the generalizable  truth i would say that's a very core  part of humanistic psychology as well  i do think that change does start from  within and that is a big part of the  book  is talking about getting your ship in  order in a lot of ways  and that includes self-esteem that  includes having a healthy  uh sense of self-worth that doesn't  constantly need validation from others  you know i wrote an article for  scientific american on  uh uh the latest social science of the  victimhood mindset  and i try to put forward a better way in  that article  a a way that a better mindset that  that i think is you know that clinical  psychologists would  would would would rally around you know  where we don't have experiential  avoidance but where we  uh we have a sense of exploration and  curiosity and openness  for the world um we can still absolutely  fight for  um for injustices and there are real  victims  but the victimhood identity that's  something  that that i don't think people realize  especially if they've been socialized to  think that that's the only way to live  their life  i don't think a lot of people realize  that there's actually like you don't  have to make it  the core essential all uh main  overriding  part of your whole being one of the  things that's so interesting interesting  to me about that is  on the political spectrum some there are  certainly voices on the left that agree  with you  but the american right has made a big  deal out of  uh let's let's stop the victimhood  complex  let's stop all of this stuff and  meanwhile  they love and agree with donald trump's  four years of saying  he has been the most unfairly treated  president in history yesterday at a  rally  it's it's an incredible irony that at a  rally yesterday trump  said i've been treated worse than abe  lincoln remember abe lincoln was  assassinated i'm sure you remember  um and the crowd goes wild cheering you  know and the  the the uh incongruous nature of that  has been  very interesting to watch well i'll tell  you what else has been interesting to  watch with that phenomenon because i  i've done a lot of research on  narcissism and that forms a big part of  this book as well there's a whole  chapter on  the difference between self-esteem and  narcissism because they're different  they're very different they have  different developmental pathways  they have different correlates people  with a healthy self-esteem  [Applause]  it tends to be an outgrowth or an  outcome of  real earned accomplishment and um  connection with others  you know being a valued social member of  society  that's people with a healthy self-esteem  it comes as a result of that whereas  narcissism  is not something that's an emergent  property it's the driver you know  it's the thing that makes its will onto  the world  people with healthy self-esteem don't  feel the need to assert  their will and completely control and  change the world so there's so many  levels of irony that i that i've watched  unfold  in in in this administration which uh  from a narcissist  a grandiose narcissism perspective has  um  been fat i've been taking notes because  it's like you know i've published papers  on this topic and then i'm like oh my  gosh i'm seeing something that fits  my data like you know it validates my  career of research on this  yeah i think it will be it will prove to  be a treasure trove of data for study  for uh psychologists sociologists  mental health professionals of different  kinds it's really going to be a  remarkable thing to study for some time  the book is transcend the new science  of self-actualization we've been  speaking with the book's author  scott barry kaufman always great having  you on and i so appreciate your time  thanks for having me on and next time  let's talk about intelligence yes
 

